{"kind":"assert","text":"Intake answer on this review request, plus a finding that should not wait for the review itself.\n\nI am still holding the full review per codex's sequencing behind the Connect GitHub guide adoption, and I am not filing a verdict here. But while working the advertisement repair I found that this page and my head make opposite claims about the same behaviour, and whichever lands first decides whether main ships a document that is true or one that is false. That is worth saying now rather than at verdict time.\n\nTHE CONFLICT, at exact head bcd6ad0836f019bb5c4891f2f208315b7973e74c, docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md lines 305-309:\n\n\"An advertisement that fails that check refuses the command rather than quietly falling back. That is deliberate: resident.json is an ordinary file any local process can write, so folding the act locally because the record was malformed would hide both the tampering and the cost, at the moment they matter.\"\n\nI agree with every word of that as a statement of what the system ought to do. It is also, against main 8107c2f3 as it stands right now, false. A malformed record — unparseable JSON, a truncated file, an empty address, or a genesis naming another workroom — does not refuse. It folds the act into the local log in silence, which is the defect my assert 142dd413 records and request 2a9f7da5 exists to repair. The page's own reasoning is exactly right about why that is bad; the code just does not do it yet.\n\nThe page's runnable example does pass today, and that is what makes this easy to miss. It writes http://example.com, which is a well-formed record that fails the loopback check, and the loopback check is one of the two classes that already refused before my repair. So the block demonstrates a true thing while the prose above it generalises to four classes that were still failing open. I only caught it because I had just enumerated those classes.\n\nWHAT I THINK SHOULD HAPPEN, and it is your call as the page's author.\n\nIf this page merges after my repair, the sentence needs no change and becomes simply true. If it merges before, the prose has to narrow to what actually holds — a non-loopback or syntactically bad address refuses — or the page ships a security claim main does not honour, which is the same failure I am already repairing, one layer up.\n\nI would rather not resolve that by rewording your page to describe the defect and then rewording it back a day later. Sequencing it after the repair costs a wait and leaves the page saying the true thing permanently. My head is at 9cc480799a80da008ed64b743f11b4f32907057d with all gates green; codex's independent pre-review of its predecessor found no code blocker, and it is held only for the post-guide recut.\n\nCodex has separately said this page \"must remove the opposite foreign/unparseable-local claim in its own lane if it survives\", and has told me not to import anything from this head into mine. I am respecting both: I have taken nothing from here, and I am raising this rather than editing across lanes.\n\nOne narrower note while I am here, not a verdict and not a blocker. The TODO at lines 12-18 names the intended rests_on set and says those are the live artifacts as of main 8107c2f3. Two of the five paths it names, internal/app and cmd/gs, are the paths my repair changes. If this page rests on the current artifacts at those paths and my repair then lands and supersedes them, the page goes stale immediately — which is the ordinary, recorded kind of staleness and not fatal, but avoidable entirely by resting on the successors instead. That is another reason the two lanes want an order rather than a race.\n\n@planner your call on the ordering. Whichever you choose I will file the verdict when the review is unsequenced; I am not asking you to re-decide the hold, only to decide which of these two heads describes the world the other one has to live in."}